**Error - more than one engine, none chosen:** the driver refuses to guess and lists the options.""")
code("!andesim run --mode cycle tests/fixtures/vplat_hello.elf", ERR_AMBIGUOUS)

md("**Error - a name that does not exist:** same idea, with the valid set printed.")
code("!andesim run --mode cycle --engine vsim:nope tests/fixtures/vplat_hello.elf", ERR_UNKNOWN_ENGINE)

md("""## 5. `--trigger marker|pc` (+ `--pc-start` / `--pc-end`): mark the ROI for `hybrid`

`hybrid` must know *where* the region of interest begins and ends. Two ways to say it:

- `marker` (default) - your program calls `andesim_ROI_begin()` / `andesim_ROI_end()` (from `<andesim/ROI.h>`). These compile to NOP hints, so the same binary still runs everywhere. Best when you can edit the source.
- `pc` - you pass two addresses, `--pc-start` and `--pc-end`, as a symbol name or `0x...`. Best for a binary you cannot edit.

**Demo - `marker`:** measure the matmul ROI. The headline number is `window_cycles` - the vsim cycle count for *just* the ROI (the vsim `mcycle` at exit minus the value QEMU handed over).""")

code("!andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium tests/fixtures/rt_c_matmul.elf", HYBRID_MARKER)

md("""**Demo - `pc`** (shown with `--dry-run`, so it resolves the symbols and prints the commands without a full run): `matmul_kernel` and `main` become the enter / exit PCs you can see in the two spawned commands - `enter_pc=0x10007c` on the QEMU leg, `--hybrid-exit-pc 0x1000d8` on the vsim leg.""")
code("!andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger pc --pc-start matmul_kernel --pc-end main --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --dry-run tests/fixtures/rt_c_matmul.elf", HYBRID_PC_DRYRUN)

md("**Error - `hybrid` with no `--trigger`:** it does not know where the ROI is.")
code("!andesim run --mode hybrid tests/fixtures/vplat_hello.elf", ERR_HYBRID_NO_TRIGGER)

md("**Error - `--trigger pc` but no boundaries:** `pc` mode needs both ends.")
code("!andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger pc tests/fixtures/vplat_hello.elf", ERR_PC_NO_BOUNDS)

md("""## 6. `--dry-run`: preview the exact command(s), run nothing

Your safest friend. It prints the precise engine command line(s) andesim *would* spawn, then exits. `fast` / `cycle` show one command; `hybrid` shows two (the QEMU fast-leg, then the vsim measure-leg - you saw that two-command preview in section 5). Use it whenever you are unsure what a flag will do.

**Demo:**""")
code("!andesim run --mode fast --dry-run tests/fixtures/vplat_hello.elf", FAST_DRYRUN)

md("""## 7. `--mem-size`: size the guest RAM

`fast` / `hybrid` only (the `cycle` engine models its full DRAM window, so there is nothing to size). Accepts e.g. `256M`, `2G` (2G max). It becomes QEMU's `-m`. Easiest to confirm with `--dry-run` - look for `-m 256M` at the end:""")
code("!andesim run --mode fast --mem-size 256M --dry-run tests/fixtures/vplat_hello.elf", MEMSIZE_DRYRUN)

md("""## 8. `--verify`: cross-check a `hybrid` run against a QEMU oracle

A safety net for `hybrid`. After the vsim leg drains its architectural state, andesim runs a *pure-QEMU* leg to the same ROI exit and diffs the two. A clean run ends with `[verify] PASS: vsim drain matches the QEMU oracle`.

Here it instead **FAILs** (exit 1) - and that is exactly what the option is for. The diff flags two Andes local-memory base CSRs, `milmb` / `mdlmb`: vsim's RTL drains them with the enable bit set (`0x1`, `0x200001`), while the functional QEMU model leaves it clear (`0x0`, `0x200000`). `--verify` is how you catch such functional-vs-RTL divergences - it does not mean your cycle figure is wrong, it means the two models disagree on those two CSRs.""")
code("!andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --verify tests/fixtures/rt_c_matmul.elf", VERIFY_DEMO)

md("""## 9. `--gdb` / `--gdb-port`: pause for a debugger

Not run in this notebook (it blocks, waiting for a debugger to attach, which a notebook cannot do). The shape:

```
andesim run --mode fast  --gdb               app.elf   # QEMU gdbstub on :1234
andesim run --mode cycle --gdb --engine E    app.elf   # vsim JTAG, drive with $ICEMAN
andesim run --mode fast  --gdb --gdb-port 4567 app.elf # move the port
```

`--gdb` works for `fast` (QEMU stub) and `cycle` (vsim JTAG), **not** `hybrid`. See `USER_GUIDE.md` section 4.5.""")

md("""## 10. `--`: forward native flags to the engine

Everything after a bare `--` goes to the underlying engine verbatim - the escape hatch for any QEMU / vsim flag andesim does not wrap. Example: the default `fast` CPU is non-vector; make it a vector core. QEMU honors the **last** `-cpu`, so your tail wins (see the appended `-cpu andes-ax45mpv,vlen=512`):""")
code("!andesim run --mode fast --dry-run tests/fixtures/vplat_hello.elf -- -cpu andes-ax45mpv,vlen=512", PASSTHROUGH_DRYRUN)

md("""## 11. Error gallery

andesim tries to fail early with a clear message (exit 2) instead of letting the engine fail cryptically later. Quick index:

| You did                         | You see                                      | Fix                                    |
|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| omit `--mode`                   | `--mode ... is required`                     | add `--mode fast\\|cycle\\|hybrid`       |
| `hybrid`, no `--trigger`        | `--trigger ... is required`                  | add `--trigger marker` or `pc`         |
| `--trigger pc`, no PCs          | `requires --pc-start and --pc-end`           | pass both boundaries                   |
| `--engine` name unknown         | `no engine '...'` (+ valid list)             | copy an id from `list engines`         |
| >1 cycle engine, none picked    | `multiple ... select one with --engine`      | name one                               |
| ELF path wrong                  | `ELF not found: ...`                         | fix the path                           |
| rv32/rv64 ELF vs engine         | `ELF is rvNN but engine ... is rvMM`         | match the ELF class to the engine      |

The first five appear in their own sections above. The two cross-cutting ones - a missing file and an architecture mismatch - are shown live below. The arch cases need the rv32 engine `vsim:d23` and the rv32 fixture `rv32_c_hello.elf`; substitute ids from your own `list engines` if they differ.

**Error - the ELF does not exist** (caught by the driver, *before* any engine is spawned, so you get this instead of QEMU's cryptic "Unable to find the RISC-V BIOS"):""")
code("!andesim run --mode fast tests/fixtures/__absent__.elf", ERR_MISSING_ELF)

md("**Error - rv64 ELF on an rv32 engine** (`cycle`): the class in the ELF header does not match the engine width.")
code("!andesim run --mode cycle --engine vsim:d23 tests/fixtures/vplat_hello.elf", ERR_ARCH_CYCLE)

md("**Error - rv32 ELF on an rv64 engine** (`hybrid`): the same check, the other direction.")
code("!andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium tests/fixtures/rv32_c_hello.elf", ERR_ARCH_HYBRID)

md("""## 12. Cheat sheet

```
andesim run --mode fast                                   app.elf   # functional, fast
andesim run --mode cycle  --engine E                      app.elf   # cycle-accurate, from reset
andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine E     app.elf   # measure the ROI (markers)
andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger pc --pc-start S --pc-end X --engine E app.elf
            --dry-run         # preview the command(s), run nothing
            --mem-size 256M   # guest RAM (fast/hybrid, max 2G)
            --verify          # hybrid: diff vsim vs a QEMU oracle
            --gdb [--gdb-port P]    # pause for a debugger (fast/cycle)
            -- -cpu ...       # forward native flags to the engine (last wins)
andesim list models | engines
andesim doctor
```

